# Difference between contract testing and api testing

API testing verifies whether an API works properly at the functional, performance, and security levels.

Whereas contract testing is a lightweight subset of API testing that verifies two services adhere to 
an agreed-upon communication format (a "contract") without needing a live, end-to-end environment.
  
In short, API testing checks if the API does the right thing, while contract testing checks if the API speaks the right language.


# Key Differences

Feature	                        API Testing	                                                                          Contract Testing

Primary Goal	   Validates business logic, data accuracy, error handling, speed, and security.	     Validates that requests and responses match an agreed schema and structure.
  
Environment	      Usually requires a running instance of the API and often connected 	               Runs in isolation using mocks or static specifications (like OpenAPI) 
                  databases or downstream services.                                                  without needing live services together.
  
Scope	           Broad. Tests single endpoints, multi-step user workflows, edge cases,               	Narrow. Focuses strictly on the interface boundaries (fields, data types, status codes)
                 and load limits.                                                                     between a consumer and a provider.

Execution Speed	 Slower (tens of milliseconds to seconds per test, depending on dependencies).	     Very fast (sub-second), making it ideal for running on every pull request in CI/CD pipelines.

Common Tools	  Postman, REST Assured, Playwright, JMeter	                                           Pact, Spring Cloud Contract, Schemathesis, OpenAPI validators

# When to Use Which

• Use API Testing when you need to verify behavior. 
It is essential for checking that a payment actually processes, an unauthorized user gets blocked, or an endpoint handles 1,000 requests per second without crashing.

• Use Contract Testing when you have independent teams or microservices. If a frontend team (consumer) and a backend team (provider) deploy on different schedules, 
contract tests immediately catch breaking changes—like renaming a JSON field—before code ever reaches a shared staging environment.

• Combine both for complete coverage. Contract tests act as fast, cheap insurance against interface drift during development.
Functional API tests then confirm the deeper business logic works as intended.
